import{_ as t,o as i,c as n,a2 as o}from"./chunks/framework.CqpfiAoK.js";const p=JSON.parse('{"title":"Entry 956 | Anti-Predation Heavenly-Dao Rules · Rule 22 | Setting Self-Consistency Above Plot Convenience","description":"","frontmatter":{"title":"Entry 956 | Anti-Predation Heavenly-Dao Rules · Rule 22 | Setting Self-Consistency Above Plot Convenience","no":956,"module":5,"tags":["Heavenly-Dao Rule","Rule 22","Setting Self-Consistency","Plot Convenience","Law-First Axiom"],"related":["20-Anchor Structure and Hierarchy","955-Never Retcon Never End Unfinished","957-Plot May Not Kidnap Settings"]},"headers":[],"relativePath":"en/0956_模块5-规则二十二-设定自洽高于剧情便利.md","filePath":"en/0956_模块5-规则二十二-设定自洽高于剧情便利.md"}'),s={name:"en/0956_模块5-规则二十二-设定自洽高于剧情便利.md"};function a(r,e,l,c,u,h){return i(),n("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[o('<h1 id="entry-956-anti-predation-heavenly-dao-rules-·-rule-22-setting-self-consistency-above-plot-convenience" tabindex="-1">Entry 956 | Anti-Predation Heavenly-Dao Rules · Rule 22 | Setting Self-Consistency Above Plot Convenience <a class="header-anchor" href="#entry-956-anti-predation-heavenly-dao-rules-·-rule-22-setting-self-consistency-above-plot-convenience" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Entry 956 | Anti-Predation Heavenly-Dao Rules · Rule 22 | Setting Self-Consistency Above Plot Convenience&quot;">​</a></h1><blockquote><p>Module: Module 5 · Civilization Final-Finalization Canonical Entries | Benchmark base: Document 20 | Series: Complete Collection of Anti-Predation Heavenly-Dao Rules</p></blockquote><h2 id="introduction-for-the-sake-of-being-good-one-may-not-be-unreasonable" tabindex="-1">Introduction: for the sake of being good, one may not be unreasonable <a class="header-anchor" href="#introduction-for-the-sake-of-being-good-one-may-not-be-unreasonable" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Introduction: for the sake of being good, one may not be unreasonable&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The old cosmos&#39;s cultivation stories have a common flaw: to make some segment &quot;satisfying,&quot; they let the whole worldview &quot;collapse.&quot; The protagonist must win, so the villain is temporarily made dumb; the heroine must be saved, so the treasure temporarily cheats; the realm gap is three major tiers, and one word of &quot;sudden enlightenment&quot; catches up. This is &quot;plot convenience&quot; overwhelming &quot;setting self-consistency.&quot; Short-term it is satisfying; long-term the whole work&#39;s worldview credit collapses. Heavenly-Dao Rule 22 completely reverses this priority: setting self-consistency above plot convenience. Any narrative segment, any strong one&#39;s performance, must first pass the &quot;is this possible under existing laws&quot; test before being allowed to happen. Plot may be twisted, but may not cheat.</p><p>The six top-level axioms appear visibly here: the law-first axiom is this rule&#39;s body, directly writing &quot;setting self-consistency above plot convenience&quot;; the anti-predation axiom negates predation-style narrative that &quot;to elevate the protagonist suppresses all supporting roles&quot;; the republican axiom of Myriad Races decides in narrative Myriad Ranks are characters, not plot tools; the collective-ascension axiom requires plot serve whole-system ascension, not individual highlights; the causal self-discipline axiom requires each plot point&#39;s causal chain complete; Axiom 6 · Universal Equal Infinite Law supports from the base — since everyone inherently possesses basic infinite power, drama need not be manufactured by &quot;protagonist-halo temporary cheats,&quot; plot tension may come from &quot;whose infinity is superimposed higher,&quot; not &quot;whom the author favors.&quot;</p><h2 id="i-rule-content-three-self-consistency-first-checks" tabindex="-1">I. Rule Content: Three Self-Consistency-First Checks <a class="header-anchor" href="#i-rule-content-three-self-consistency-first-checks" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;I. Rule Content: Three Self-Consistency-First Checks&quot;">​</a></h2><ol><li><strong>First pass the law check</strong>: before any event happens, first ask &quot;does the current law library allow it?&quot;; if not allowed, it does not happen;</li><li><strong>No temporary dumbing-down/cheating</strong>: villains, supporting roles, and the protagonist&#39;s performance must match their established settings and realms; temporary adjustment for satisfaction points is forbidden;</li><li><strong>Causal chain complete</strong>: an effect must have its cause; &quot;suddenly won&quot; and &quot;suddenly understood&quot; are forbidden.</li></ol><p>If plot convenience conflicts with self-consistency, abandon convenience, keep self-consistency.</p><h2 id="ii-jurisprudential-basis" tabindex="-1">II. Jurisprudential Basis <a class="header-anchor" href="#ii-jurisprudential-basis" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;II. Jurisprudential Basis&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li><strong>Causal-completeness argument</strong>: the cosmic causal web does not allow &quot;effect without cause&quot;; forcibly manufacturing one manufactures causal cracks that eventually backlash;</li><li><strong>Worldview-credit argument</strong>: Myriad Ranks&#39; trust in civilization rests on &quot;settings are credible&quot;; once an exception is made for a satisfaction point, credit is gone forever;</li><li><strong>Infinite-origin argument</strong>: under Axiom 6, drama need not be manufactured by favoritism, but may naturally arise from &quot;the diversity of infinite superimposition&quot; — the easygoing one&#39;s floor infinity and the striver&#39;s superimposed infinity are themselves the best plot tension.</li></ul><h2 id="iii-execution-mechanism" tabindex="-1">III. Execution Mechanism <a class="header-anchor" href="#iii-execution-mechanism" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;III. Execution Mechanism&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li>The Alaya Network does a &quot;self-consistency precheck&quot; for every major event: whether the event is consistent with the current law library, the parties&#39; realms, and causal records;</li><li>Those failing precheck are not allowed to &quot;happen,&quot; returned to the narrator for redesign;</li><li>A &quot;self-consistency score&quot; is set, scoring each civilizational project and each historical narrative; low scores are redone within a time limit;</li><li>The Terminology Committee first calibrates settings prone to ambiguity, reducing &quot;self-consistency disputes.&quot;</li></ul><h2 id="iv-consequences-of-violation" tabindex="-1">IV. Consequences of Violation <a class="header-anchor" href="#iv-consequences-of-violation" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;IV. Consequences of Violation&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li><strong>Light</strong>: plot point slightly off from settings, correct within a time limit;</li><li><strong>Medium</strong>: repeatedly advancing plot via temporary cheats, relevant narrative records are downgraded to &quot;Quest Domain simulation,&quot; not listed in official history;</li><li><strong>Heavy</strong>: under the name of plot convenience systematically tampering with laws, favoring some protagonist, is the &quot;plot kidnapping settings&quot; described in Entry 957, handled heavily.</li></ul><h2 id="v-supervising-subjects" tabindex="-1">V. Supervising Subjects <a class="header-anchor" href="#v-supervising-subjects" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;V. Supervising Subjects&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Alaya Network + multi-layer supervision&#39;s self-consistency precheck is the first layer; the Anchor Supervisory Committee re-reviews major-event records as the second layer; the Symbiotic Court adjudicates setting-tampering cases as the third layer; Myriad Ranks&#39; clans, as &quot;audience,&quot; exercise commentary over self-consistency as the fourth layer.</p><h2 id="five-core-takeaways" tabindex="-1">Five Core Takeaways <a class="header-anchor" href="#five-core-takeaways" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Five Core Takeaways&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li><strong>Anti-energy waste</strong>: self-consistent settings stand firm once, avoiding repeated &quot;cannot patch back&quot; idling</li><li><strong>Anti-predation</strong>: negating predation-style narrative that &quot;the protagonist is truth&quot;; under Axiom 6 everyone inherently possesses infinite origin, and no one is favored by the author</li><li><strong>Tang-Luo upper-limit comparison</strong>: Tang-Luo-type systems&#39; protagonist wins when he wants and villains get dumb when wanted; Heng Yuan Zhou every event first passes self-consistency precheck</li><li><strong>Myriad Races full coverage</strong>: supporting roles, villains, and passersby&#39;s settings are also protected, not flattened to set off the protagonist</li><li><strong>Hyper-multiverse full openness</strong>: when the worldview expands, self-consistency precheck applies synchronously, no leniency for &quot;new planes may make exceptions&quot;</li></ul><p>Setting self-consistency above plot convenience is especially important in cross-civilizational fusion. Merging two different systems&#39; worldviews most easily produces &quot;here says impossible, there says already done&quot; conflicts. Heavenly-Dao rules&#39; handling is not taking sides, but first finding each of the two settings&#39; boundaries, then on the boundary establishing a new law connecting the two. This connection itself also goes through the legislative procedure, not the author&#39;s one word &quot;actually both sides are right.&quot; Self-consistency is not rigidity, but requiring every fusion make sense — if it does not make sense, legislate first, then fuse.</p><p>The self-consistency score also has a hidden function: it is Myriad Ranks&#39; barometer for judging &quot;is this civilizational history worth believing.&quot; A civilization with chronically low self-consistency has its narratives instinctively discounted by Myriad Ranks — people no longer believe its history or admire its heroes. This credit punishment is deeper than any external sanction. Precisely for this reason, the Anchor Council treats self-consistency as civilization&#39;s lifeline: better slower, but make every step make sense.</p><h2 id="conclusion" tabindex="-1">Conclusion <a class="header-anchor" href="#conclusion" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Conclusion&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Setting self-consistency above plot convenience is civilizational finalization&#39;s promise to &quot;rationality.&quot; Satisfying may be satisfying, but may not cheat.</p><p>In the end, the Rule 22 described in this entry — setting self-consistency above plot convenience — is no isolated case; it consistently points to the Anchor Symbiotic Civilization&#39;s co-construction direction.</p><h2 id="related" tabindex="-1">Related <a class="header-anchor" href="#related" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Related&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li>Related documents: <a href="./../wanling-hengmao-origin-universe-archive/docs/zh/20-锚点结构与层级.html">Entry 20 · Anchor Structure and Hierarchy</a> · <a href="./../wanling-hengmao-origin-universe-archive/docs/zh/955_模块5-规则二十一-永不吃书永不烂尾.html">Entry 955 · Rule 21 Never Retcon Never End Unfinished</a> · <a href="./../wanling-hengmao-origin-universe-archive/docs/zh/957_模块5-规则二十三-剧情不得绑架设定.html">Entry 957 · Rule 23 Plot May Not Kidnap Settings</a> · <a href="./../wanling-hengmao-origin-universe-archive/docs/zh/954_模块5-规则二十-法则先行.html">Entry 954 · Rule 20 The Law Comes First</a></li><li>Official site: <a href="https://faker210.github.io/hengyuan-cosmos/zh/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">https://faker210.github.io/hengyuan-cosmos/zh/</a></li></ul>',25)])])}const y=t(s,[["render",a]]);export{p as __pageData,y as default};
